\f0\fs22 \cf2 In the paperwork it was a flow event.\
There had been eleven gates and four of them were staffed, because staffing all eleven had been costed at an amount the operator described, in a submission later read aloud at the inquiry to general agreement, as difficult to justify against projected attendance. Projected attendance had been calculated by the same department. Everybody funnelled to the four gates that worked. The four gates that worked were fitted with barriers designed to prevent people entering the wrong way, which they did, superbly, for the entire duration.\
The inquiry found that the deceased had failed to disperse in an orderly manner. It recommended better signage.\
What nobody had put in a submission, because nobody in an office had ever needed to know it, was that a crowd pressed hard enough does not fall over. There is nowhere to fall. It stands. And when it stops, it goes on standing, and some hours later it sets, and by the time anyone with the authority to sign anything arrives at the site, what is waiting for them is not a heap. It is a wall, and the wall is made of people, and they are all still on their feet.\
Chanta was in the fourth crew in.\
The first three had tried it properly. Two-man teams, gloves, straps, a chaplain who had been rung at four in the morning and had come. They worked from the outside in for six hours and got eleven out, which they laid down on the tarpaulin in the order they came, and which the chaplain named, and which was, everybody agreed, the right way to do it.\
The problem was arithmetic. Eleven out of what the operator\u8217 ?s own turnstile data insisted was four thousand two hundred, over six hours, is a job that finishes in about eleven weeks, and there was a Sunday market booked.\
So at half past two a man in a hard hat with a clipboard walked the length of it, and made a phone call, and then made a second phone call to somebody senior enough to say the thing out loud, and at ten past three a front-end loader came up the ramp.\
Chanta\u8217 ?s crew were told to stand back from the plant and work behind it.\
That was the whole of the briefing. Behind it.\
He got through most of the afternoon by not looking up. This was a technique he had, and he was better at it than anyone else on his crew, and it was the only thing in his life he had ever been told he had a gift for. You worked a square metre. You did not look at the square metre next to yours until it was your square metre. You did not look at the loader, and you did not look at where the loader had been, and above all you did not look at the front of it, which had a shape now that it had not had at ten past three.\
At about five he stopped being able to do it.\
He got himself behind the wall of the substation at the western end, where the light was bad and the smell was mostly diesel, and put a hand on the brickwork, and was sick until there was nothing left, and then a while longer.\
When he opened his eyes there was a policeman on his knees a metre away, in the good spot, doing exactly the same thing.\
Chanta had not heard him arrive, because you don\u8217 ?t.\
The policeman was young. His uniform had never been worn; it still had the packing creases across the back and the hard edge on the collar, and the boots were a colour boots stop being in about a week. There was a rank on the shoulder. Chanta had cleaned up after the police for years and had never seen that rank on anybody, and had a vague sense that if he asked about it he would be given an answer, at length.\
Neither of them said anything for a while. It seemed fair.\
\u8220 ?There\u8217 ?s a bin,\u8221 ? Chanta offered eventually, and nodded at it, and then realised the bin was full and had been since about two, and let it go.\
The policeman waved a hand. It was a soft hand. His face was round and his eyes were small and pink at the edges and his ears had gone a colour, and up close there was something about the whole arrangement that made Chanta think, without any unkindness and without pursuing it, of an animal in a tree.\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m not supposed to be back here,\u8221 ? the policeman said.\
\u8220 ?Nobody\u8217 ?s supposed to be back here.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?No, I mean I\u8217 ?m required to be at the front. There\u8217 ?s a \u8212 ?\u8221 ? He stopped and swallowed and got it under control. \u8220 ?There\u8217 ?s a photograph at six.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Of what?\u8221 ?\
The policeman looked at him.\
\u8220 ?Right,\u8221 ? Chanta said.\
Chanta sat down on the concrete with his back against the substation wall, because his legs had made the decision without consulting him, and got out his sandwich. Clean-up crews were fed; it was in the award, and it was the only clause in the award that had never been amended.\
He had been carrying it since eleven and had not opened it. He opened it now, out of curiosity more than appetite, and it turned out that whoever catered a thing like this had decided to be generous about it.\
The policeman\u8217 ?s head came round.\
\u8220 ?Is that real beef?\u8221 ?\
Chanta looked at the sandwich. Then he held it out.\
\u8220 ?You can have it,\u8221 ? he said. \u8220 ?I don\u8217 ?t think I\u8217 ?ll need it.\u8221 ?\
The policeman took it in both hands. He ate the whole thing, sitting on his heels in the dark next to a substation, with his eyes shut, and he made a noise partway through that he was almost certainly not aware of.\
Somewhere out past the wall the loader changed gear.\
They both went very still.\
The thing about the wall was that it was two metres of brick and there was nothing to see. Chanta looked at it anyway. He kept looking at it, and he was aware, in the way you are aware of weather, that his chest had gone tight and stupid, and that he wanted very much to be somewhere with a door in it, and that the other bloke had stopped chewing.\
It went on for a bit.\
Then the loader finished whatever it was doing and idled, and the air came back into both of them at about the same time, and neither of them said anything about it, then or ever.\
The policeman stood up.\
Chanta watched him do it, and that was the part Chanta remembered, when he remembered any of it, which was not often.\
He stood up and he put his shoulders back, and he ran both hands down the front of his tunic, and then he did something with his face. It took under a second. Chanta had seen a woman in a karaoke bar do the same thing between the door and the microphone and it had taken her four steps; this took a second and a half, and it was better work.\
\u8220 ?Lieutenant Alexander Shakti,\u8221 ? said the policeman, and put out his hand, and his voice had come from somewhere else entirely. It was a beautiful voice. It sounded like the second half of a sentence you had already agreed with. \u8220 ?Public Relations. Well\u8212 ?Homicide, formally, but one wears several hats, my good fellow, one wears several hats.\u8221 ?\
Chanta looked at the hand for a moment and then shook it.\
\u8220 ?Chanta.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Chanta.\u8221 ? Alexander held on a fraction longer than the handshake needed. \u8220 ?And what is it you do here, Chanta?\u8221 ?\
Chanta gestured at his overalls. There was a logo over the breast pocket. It was upside down to Alexander and Alexander read it anyway, which meant he had already read it, before, at some point, and had asked the question anyway.\
\u8220 ?Ah,\u8221 ? Alexander said. \u8220 ?The very best sort of chap. The absolutely indispensable sort.\u8221 ? He produced a card from somewhere on his person and put it into Chanta\u8217 ?s hand, closing Chanta\u8217 ?s fingers over it with both of his own, which were still warm and did not feel like hands. \u8220 ?Now. Between us\u8212 ?and I do mean between us, Chanta\u8212 ?that is not a scene I would wish on a colleague. All right? Between us.\u8221 ?\
Chanta had not said anything to be between them.\
\u8220 ?Sure,\u8221 ? he said.\
\u8220 ?Good man. Good man.\u8221 ? Alexander was already turning towards the front, already several metres ahead of himself, already, Chanta could see, at the photograph. \u8220 ?If you\u8217 ?re ever in any trouble at all, you ring me. Any trouble. Straight to me.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Right.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I mean it, my old mate.\u8221 ? And he was gone round the corner of the substation, and half a minute later, out at the front, somebody with a good camera got a picture that ran on three networks of a young officer with his cap under his arm, looking off to the left of frame with an expression of enormous and carefully lit sorrow.\
Chanta stayed where he was until the light went.\
Then he went back and finished his square metre.\
\
The second time was seven weeks later, in an apartment eleven floors up, at a quarter to two in the afternoon.\
Chanta had been called in early, which never happened. Normally a cleaner sat in the van in the loading dock for four hours while everybody upstairs took photographs of everything, and then somebody came down and said righto, and that was that. This time the call had come while he was still on another job, and the voice on the phone had been personal about it.\
He came out of the lift and there were nine people in the corridor, and every one of them was standing in the specific way people stand when they are waiting for somebody else to be blamed. Two of them had cameras. One of them had a camera and a lanyard and a folding chair, which was ambitious.\
Alexander was in the doorway. He had a proper coat on now, and the coat had been chosen, and under it his shirt was stuck to him.\
\u8220 ?Chanta!\u8221 ? he said, with real joy, and got an arm round Chanta\u8217 ?s shoulders, and steered him inside, and shut the door on all of them.\
The flat was expensive and small. There was a man on the floor between the sofa and the coffee table, and there was a gun near his hand, and there was a note propped on the coffee table where the light from the balcony fell on it very nicely.\
\u8220 ?Right,\u8221 ? Alexander said. His voice had dropped about a foot. \u8220 ?Right. So. Talk me through what you\u8217 ?re seeing.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m the cleaner.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Yes.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?You want me to\u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I want you to talk me through what you\u8217 ?re seeing,\u8221 ? Alexander said, \u8220 ?because there are four people out in that corridor who have taken this case off me in their heads already, and my crew are downstairs, and I have to say something at four o\u8217 ?clock.\u8221 ?\
Chanta had never in his life been asked a question by somebody who needed the answer.\
He looked at the room.\
\u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s been cleaned,\u8221 ? he said.\
Alexander blinked. \u8220 ?It\u8217 ?s a crime scene.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Yeah. Someone\u8217 ?s gone over it.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?There\u8217 ?s a body on the floor.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?There\u8217 ?s a body on the floor and no dust on the skirting.\u8221 ? Chanta crouched and ran two fingers along it and showed him. \u8220 ?Have a smell of that.\u8221 ?\
Alexander smelled his own fingers afterwards and pulled a face.\
\u8220 ?That\u8217 ?s Bractol Two,\u8221 ? Chanta said. \u8220 ?Nobody has that at home. You can\u8217 ?t buy it at home. It\u8217 ?s a bond-clean product; it strips. You use it when you want the surface gone, not the mark.\u8221 ? He stood up, slowly, because his knees had been on concrete for six years. \u8220 ?Whoever did it did the low stuff and the flat stuff and missed the tops of the doors, which is what everyone does, because you have to want to look up.\u8221 ?\
Alexander looked up. There was a line of grey along the top edge of the door, undisturbed, a finger deep.\
\u8220 ?And that means?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It means somebody was in here for a couple of hours with a bucket before you got here.\u8221 ? Chanta shrugged. \u8220 ?Might be nothing. People do all sorts.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?It is not nothing,\u8221 ? Alexander said. He had gone a bit grey himself.\
\u8220 ?Also,\u8221 ? Chanta said, and this was the bit he was actually annoyed about, \u8220 ?one of your blokes has walked it out into the hall.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Alfred Deakin,\u8221 ? Alexander said. \u8220 ?Which one?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Bloke by the fire door. Sandy hair. Wipes his mouth a lot.\u8221 ? Chanta scratched his ear. \u8220 ?He\u8217 ?s got it on his soles. It goes tacky in the tread and it picks up carpet fluff, so it looks like he\u8217 ?s walked through something white. It only does that for about a day. If he was here this morning, it\u8217 ?s from here.\u8221 ? He thought about it. \u8220 ?Unless he does bond cleans on the weekend.\u8221 ?\
Alexander stood in the middle of the room for a long moment with his mouth slightly open.\
Then he opened the door and went out into the corridor, and Chanta, through the gap, heard him say, in the beautiful voice, without any hurry at all: \u8220 ?Constable\u8212 ?no, not you. You. Would you be so good as to take your boots off.\u8221 ?\
There was a silence out there that Chanta could feel through the wall.\
It took another forty minutes. When Alexander came back in he was a different shape, buoyant to the point of levitation, and he had the folding chair man\u8217 ?s camera trained on him through the doorway, and he shut the door on that too, which Chanta thought was interesting.\
\u8220 ?Chanta.\u8221 ? He seized Chanta\u8217 ?s hand and shook it with both of his. \u8220 ?Chanta. My dear fellow. That is my first. My actual, genuine, from-the-front-foot first.\u8221 ? His ears had gone the colour again. \u8220 ?Now. Listen. Name a figure.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?What?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?A figure.\u8221 ? Alexander had his tablet out and was already thumbing at it, delighted, expansive, entirely himself. \u8220 ?I have a discretionary. Nobody looks at it. Name something ridiculous, my old mate, name something obscene, I insist \u8212 ?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Nah.\u8221 ?\
Alexander stopped. \u8220 ?Nah?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Don\u8217 ?t want money.\u8221 ?\
Now here is the thing that Chanta could not have explained, and was never asked to, and would not have had the words for if he had been. He had spent his whole life being paid. Being paid was the transaction that ended a thing. You did the work, they gave you the money, and after that they didn\u8217 ?t know you, and the following week you needed work again.\
\u8220 ?One day,\u8221 ? Chanta said, \u8220 ?I\u8217 ?ll ask you for something. And you\u8217 ?ll do it. No questions, no bill.\u8221 ?\
Alexander\u8217 ?s face did the thing where it decided to be moved.\
\u8220 ?Chanta,\u8221 ? he said.\
\u8220 ?Yeah.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Chanta.\u8221 ? He put his hand on Chanta\u8217 ?s shoulder and left it there, and his eyes had gone shiny, and he was, in that moment, absolutely and completely sincere, which was the trouble with him. \u8220 ?Done. Done and done. Anything. Anything at all, at any hour.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Right.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I mean it.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I know you do.\u8221 ?\
Alexander laughed, hugely, like a man who has got away with something and is glad the world is such a place, and put his tablet away without spending a cent of the discretionary, and went out into the corridor to say the thing at four o\u8217 ?clock.\
The following Tuesday, a call came through for a deep clean at an address in the east, from the Department of Traffic Movement and Property Security, Homicide, and the account was set up before Chanta had put the phone down.\
There have been rather a lot of them since.\
\
The remote job came the following autumn, and it is the reason a certain generation of officers still refer to Alexander Shakti, in private, as the best natural detective who ever came out of the Roger Rogerson Police Academy.\
The set-up was Chanta\u8217 ?s and it was extremely simple, because complicated things went wrong and Chanta had been at enough sites to know who got blamed when they did. Alexander wore camera glasses, which he owned already, for reasons. Alexander wore an earpiece, which he also owned already, for the same reasons. Chanta sat in the back of the van two streets away with a monitor and a tuna roll.\
\u8220 ?Ask him how long the window\u8217 ?s been open,\u8221 ? Chanta said.\
\u8220 ?How long has that window been open?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Say it like you already know.\u8221 ?\
Alexander said it again, like he already knew.\
The room went quiet, and a man in a lab coat looked at another man in a lab coat, and something happened in the doorway that Chanta could not see from the angle he had, but could hear, and which he later described to nobody as the sound of a department changing its mind about somebody.\
It went on for two hours and eleven minutes.\
Chanta gave him the salient question, and gave it early, because you get one. He gave him the resource allocation, which is only ever a matter of knowing which two people in a room actually do the work; Chanta had watched that in every building he had ever mopped. He gave him a joke for the forensic team about a bloke who once tried to charge him for parking at a triple homicide, which killed, and which the forensic team repeated for years, and which is the reason four of them still said hello to him in lifts for the rest of his career.\
And near the end, when a senior sergeant with an entire career\u8217 ?s worth of grievance said something quiet and clever from behind a doorframe, Chanta chewed his roll and said, \u8220 ?Tell him to get fucked.\u8221 ?\
There was a pause on the line.\
\u8220 ?I beg your pardon?\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Say: get fucked, Barry.\u8221 ?\
Another pause, of a length that Chanta, in the van, could feel in his teeth.\
Then, in the beautiful voice, at a volume calibrated to carry to precisely the back of the room and no further:\
\u8220 ?Get fucked, Barry.\u8221 ?\
Chanta heard the noise the room made. He heard it through an earpiece microphone two streets away in the back of a van and he had to put the roll down.\
In the room, Alexander Shakti stood very still in the middle of a homicide scene with his own words still in the air, and discovered, at an age by which most men have stopped noticing, something that had been available to him the entire time.\
\u8220 ?Alex,\u8221 ? Chanta said. \u8220 ?Alex. Mate. Don\u8217 ?t do it again.\u8221 ?\
He did it again.}
